    {
      "title": "South Korea to re-examine 310,000 missing person cases after fake record scandal",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/28/south-korea-missing-persons-police-scandal-bodies-jeju",
      "description": "<p>Renewed scrutiny comes after police officer accused of falsifying records in order to close the cases of people who were later found dead</p><p>South Korean police will re-examine about 310,000 missing persons cases closed over the past three years, prompted by a scandal in which an officer was arrested on suspicion of falsifying records to close files on two people who were later found dead.</p><p>The case has drawn national attention, with President Lee Jae Myung <a href=\"https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10851414\">ordering an investigation</a> into the police handling of it and telling the force to draw up a reform plan.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/28/south-korea-missing-persons-police-scandal-bodies-jeju\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-08-28T03:06:40"
    },
